 "Lydian ambient soundscape layered by frequency range: lower-mid choir chords, mid noise-loop pad, dark saw bass and upper-mid granular/reversed piano sparkles, each checked with a mute test, then arranged by fade-ins, slowly opening filters and an automated reverb mix that merges everything into a cloud",
 "Build a lush ambient soundscape at 70 BPM in a Lydian key by assigning each layer a frequency range rather than stacking similar pads. Write a 16-bar extended-chord cadence (Cmaj7 → Cadd#11 with the #11 a semitone from the 5th an octave down for gentle dissonance → back to C with the 9th), voice it on a soft choir, bounce it, then filter + EQ + big convolution reverb (lower mids). Add a mid pad: single noise-loop oscillator, soft attack and release, lowpass, a sparser two-note voicing of the same changes an octave up, a slow wandering LFO on noise rate/fade, slight detune spread, tube saturation whose drive is modulated by random noise, a phaser for wash and a huge reverb. Add a dark warm bass (two saws, gradually opening envelope, noise-modulated saturation, chorus, lowpass). Cover the upper mids/highs with a looped granular piano one-shot (small scan, mid grain size, EQ, echo) playing chord tones, bounced and reversed for reverse hits through a big boost EQ, echo and a very large reverb, plus a textured sample through the same chain. Mute-test every layer; keep only the ones you can still hear adding something. Arrange as a slow-evolving piece: bounce layers to audio, fade the intro in, sprinkle unreversed sparkles, slowly open each layer's lowpass over the whole track, and automate the reverb mix on the reversed layer from dry-ish to fully wet so it ends as a sparkly cloud. Polyrhythm Studio: per-layer band roles + long filter/reverb-mix control lanes.",
